  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
